<section class="block">
<h2>What was agreed</h2>
<p>OpenAI, Google, Microsoft and Anthropic said on 30 October 2026 that they will honour one shared robots.txt token for opting out of AI training, so a single rule can cover all four companies' crawlers; the token is described in an IETF Internet-Draft<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. It builds on the IETF's wider work on a shared vocabulary for AI usage preferences<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The details matter more than the headline. Reporting says Anthropic has begun honouring the token, Microsoft is targeting early 2027 and separates training from grounding with a different tag, Google plans to treat the opt-out like its existing Google-Extended control, which also covers grounding for Gemini answers, and OpenAI has said it aims to implement it in the coming months<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Sources differ on some timings. The signal is voluntary and does not remove content already collected or models already trained<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Training, grounding and search are three different decisions</h2>
<p>The most important practical point is that "AI use" is not one thing. Training puts your content into a model. Grounding fetches your content at answer time and may cite and link you. Search indexing sends people to your site. Opting out of one may or may not affect the others, depending on the company.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Search indexing</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Classic results<em>traffic to your site</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Grounding</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">AI answers that cite you<em>may or may not follow the training opt-out</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Training</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Shared opt-out token<em>one rule, four companies, staggered rollout</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Enforcement</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Voluntary signals<em>robots.txt and headers</em></span><span>Your edge<em>gateway, bot management, logs</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Decide separately for training, grounding and search, then check how each company maps the token to them.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>For most businesses, the right answer differs by content type. Marketing pages and documentation usually benefit from being cited in AI answers. Paid research, proprietary datasets and member content usually should not train anyone's model for free. That is the same tiering logic as in <a href="/insights/reddit-logged-in-ai-scraping-content-access/">what Reddit's logged-in move means for content</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A practical rollout</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Classify</b><span>Group content into discovery, premium and licensed tiers</span></li>
<li><b>Decide</b><span>For each tier, choose training, grounding and search separately</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Express</b><span>Add the shared token for training opt-outs; keep company-specific rules where needed</span></li>
<li><b>Enforce</b><span>Back premium tiers with sign-in, rate limits and bot management</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Verify</b><span>Monitor crawler traffic by user agent and path to confirm behaviour</span></li>
<li><b>Review</b><span>Recheck as each company's rollout date arrives</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>The token is one step in a content access policy, not the whole policy.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>Keep the old rules for now.</strong> Until every company has implemented the shared token, keep existing company-specific crawler rules in place; reporting says those continue to work<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>Do not rely on signals for valuable content.</strong> Voluntary signals are honoured by companies that choose to honour them. Content that is your competitive asset needs technical controls at your edge, as described in the <a href="/case-studies/indonesia-ewallet-payment-page-script-security/">front-end controls</a> and gateway patterns elsewhere on this site.</p>
<p><strong>Verify, do not assume.</strong> Log requests by user agent and path at your edge or in your traces, and check that crawlers stop fetching opted-out sections after the relevant rollout dates. Enriching traces with request headers makes this a simple filter rather than a log-parsing project<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/shared-ai-training-opt-out-robots-txt/header-filtered-traces.webp" alt="Jaeger trace search filtered by a request header attribute, showing a scatter plot of trace durations and a list of matching checkout traces" width="1400" height="1062" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Filtering traffic by request header: the same technique shows whether a given crawler still fetches opted-out paths. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/obi-http-header-enrichment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Tyler Yahn and Nimrod Avni</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>A robots.txt line is a request, not a lock. Use it to state your preferences clearly, and use your own infrastructure for anything you cannot afford to lose.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Who should act</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Publishers, marketplaces and data businesses</strong>: decide per content tier now, so the token goes in with a clear policy behind it.</li>
<li><strong>B2B SaaS and documentation-heavy companies</strong>: think twice before opting out of grounding; being cited in AI answers is increasingly how buyers find you. See <a href="/insights/structured-data-for-ai-search/">structured data for AI search</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Regulated organisations in the EU, UK and elsewhere</strong>: record your choices; they may matter for copyright and AI transparency discussions. See the <a href="/fractional-cto-europe/">Europe page</a>.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>A single opt-out signal backed by four major AI companies is genuinely useful: it lowers the cost of saying no to training. But it arrives in stages, it does not cover the same things at each company, and it remains voluntary. Treat it as one part of a content access policy that separates training, grounding and search, enforces what matters at your edge, and verifies behaviour with your own data. Read alongside <a href="/insights/reddit-logged-in-ai-scraping-content-access/">the Reddit analysis</a> and <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
</section>

